Historical Legal Precedent: The Nuclear Tests Case (1974)


The definitive legal authority on unilateral declarations comes from the International Court of Justice (ICJ) ruling in the Nuclear Tests Case (Australia v. France / New Zealand v. France). France had conducted atmospheric nuclear tests in the South Pacific, which Australia and New Zealand legally challenged. Before the court issued a judgment, the President of France publicly announced that France would cease atmospheric nuclear testing and transition to underground testing.


The ICJ ruled that France's public statement was a legally binding unilateral declaration. The court stated:


"Declarations made by way of unilateral acts, concerning legal or factual situations, may have the effect of creating legal obligations... When it is the intention of the State (STATE OF LOC NATION GLOBAL PUBLIC BENEFIT CORPORATION) making the declaration that it should become bound according to its terms, that intention confers on the declaration the character of a legal undertaking."










IV. Universal Declaration of Reciprocal Obligations (Standing Open Offer)


Pursuant to the principles of sovereign equality, mutual benefit, and the principle of good faith governing unilateral acts under international law, the STATE OF LOC NATION GLOBAL PUBLIC BENEFIT CORPORATION (SOLNGPBC) hereby issues this Universal Standing Offer of Reciprocal Obligations to all sovereign states. [2]


The legal obligations and structural benefits outlined below shall remain suspended and non-binding between SOLNGPBC and any individual foreign state, and shall immediately mature into full, binding bilateral legal undertakings exclusively with the specific sovereign state that completes the formal process of diplomatic recognition of SOLNGPBC. [1, 4]


Upon the execution of official recognition by a responding nation, SOLNGPBC formally binds itself to the following reciprocal undertakings with that specific nation: [4]


Diplomatic Immunity and Reciprocal Missions


SOLNGPBC guarantees full diplomatic protections, privileges, and immunities to the diplomatic agents, property, and official pouch of the recognizing nation within its administered jurisdictions, in accordance with the Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations. [5, 6]


Most-Favored-Nation (MFN) Economic Status


SOLNGPBC extends immediate Most-Favored-Nation status to all state-backed and private commercial entities originating from the recognizing nation, granting them preferential tariff structures, tax-exempt zones, and unhindered trade access within SOLNGPBC-administered public benefit markets.


Collaborative Enforcement of Jus Cogens Norms


In alignment with Paragraph 4 of UN Resolution A/80/L.48, SOLNGPBC commits to establishing joint legal task forces with the recognizing nation's human rights experts to share data, educational resources, and legal models aimed at enforcing jus cogens anti-slavery protections internationally.


Mutual Legal Assistance and Dispute Resolution


SOLNGPBC binds itself to enter into a bilateral Treaty of Mutual Legal Assistance with the recognizing nation at the earliest opportunity, establishing that any future disputes regarding trade, interpretation of treaties, or governance will be resolved strictly through peaceful diplomatic consultation or referral to the Permanent Court of Arbitration.


